      ":question/id": "9ebf58d1-5742-57e5-a496-12b3c24f7243",
      ":question/problem": "Find $k$, if $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0$ and\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the properties of addition/subtraction and scalar multiplication of vectors to isolate the $k$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2} & =0 \\\\ 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+k\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ k\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the second components, we get\n\n$$\n4k=-12\\,⟹\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nBut then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}\\ne \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -12 \\\\ -18\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is no $k$ such that $2{a}_{1}+k{a}_{2}=0.$",
